A||N||8||bHooper||the right rear cradle bolt won't tighten!||||4||
Z||000000||bHooper||05-07-2000||06:01 PM||||so, I'm wrappin up work on the GT and went to install the cradle.  I started with the rear ones first, and the right rear bolt will not tighten down.<P>The "nut" inside the frame has a lot of play with it, and no matter which way I turn it, it doesnt tighten!<P>Any suggestions, short of cutting the frame, will be taken as welcome advice.<P>This could have been the reason there wasnt a cradle bolt there when I took the thing apart... more likely the SOB's that took the tranny out forgot to replace the bolt!<P>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||4
Z||000001||Oreif||05-07-2000||11:14 PM||||The only fix I know of it to cut a slot in the frame rail. Those nuts are usually welded in place, If you have play it's not welded anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000002||bHooper||05-08-2000||09:03 AM||||so what is involved in cutting the frame in this location?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||05-08-2000||11:40 AM||||Doing the clutch job on my Fiero, I had the same problem. The way I did it(didn't have a sawzall) was to take my electric drill and drill a "U" in the frame, allowing me to use pliers to bend the "U" open. Took 10-15 minutes. Then after tightening down the nut and bolt, I just bent the flap back down. Not a tough job to do at all. This was Ed Parks recommendation when I asked him about it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||05-08-2000||01:50 PM||||I have also tried to jack up on the car to press tension on the bolt so the nut can't turn. That seems to work. I learned this after making a hole in the frame rail  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> I would try this before I cut||||reg||3
Z||000005||bHooper||05-08-2000||03:11 PM||||also curious as to which way the washer is supposed to go... center cone up, or down.<P>this is the washer that goes between the bushing and the frame.<P><BR>thanks,<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000006||dman||05-08-2000||05:53 PM||||Hi bHooper. I tried to post this last night. It was fairly long then too but it didn't get through for some reason. Anyway here goes. <P>Not that long a ago I read a description online that described how to remove a cradle bolt that spins freely because the nut (inside the frame rail) has come undone. The article mentioned that this nut is rectangular (or at the least a collar that holds down the nut is rectangular). The article suggested that a small hole be drilled on the frame rail, a little bit away from the cradle bolt (about an inch or two? I'm guessing here). This hole should be tapped so that a bolt can be screwed into it. I think the bolt tip should be rounded a little. Tighten down the bolt and it will stop the nut from spinning around in there. I'm not sure if the bolt was supposed to clamp the nut down, or if it ended up just being in the way of the rectangular nut thereby blocking it from spinning.<P>Like I said, I read this on the internet. I've never done this myself nor am I sure it even works. I wish I could remember where I read it because I'm very interested in this technique. I may very well have read it here on the forum. <P>It sounds like it would work. I'm sure that someone here in the forum has probably got ready access to a cradle and can probably tell us whether this technique could really work.<P>Of course this was for -removing- a problem cradle bolt. If this technique actually works I see no reason why you couldn't use it to -tighten- a problem cradle bolt.<P>I hope this helps. Let us know if you try this because I am -very- interested to know if this will work since I will be dropping the cradle on a couple of my fieros in the not too distant future. It pays to know useful tricks to deal with those annoying problems.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||V8Fiero||05-09-2000||08:14 PM||||Can you get in from the back of the frame? I had this happen to me. I ended up drilling out the original bolt and nut and putting in a whole new set up. I could get in from the back of the frame and used a screwdriver to keep the nut from spinning while I turned in the bolt. On one of my other fieros the last owner must have had the same problem. They cut a slot in the frame. I would save that as a last resort. Hope this helps.||||reg||6
Z||000008||RossT||05-10-2000||03:37 AM||||Hey v8fiero, finally another forum memeber from ND!!! How many and what type of fieros do you own!! I have one 88gt and soon to have another.  Look me up next time in Bismarck, we have to get together..<P>Ross||||reg||1
